#!/bin/sh
# GF pre-commit secret guard — blocks committing rcon passwords, join passwords,
# the Plutonium server key, the gitignored secret stores, or a public IP literal
# (player PII / infrastructure disclosure). Tracked so it travels with the repo;
# enabled per-clone via:  git config core.hooksPath tools/hooks
#
# IP rule opt-outs, for an address this repo intends to publish:
#   * one line   — put the token  ip-ok  in a comment on that line (whitelists the
#                  WHOLE line, so a line carrying a second address is exempt too)
#   * repo-wide  — add the address to tools/hooks/ip-allow.txt (one per line,
#                  '#' comments allowed). Use this for an address that recurs.
#   ⚠ PREFER the allowlist the moment an address appears more than once, so 'ip-ok'
#     stays reserved for genuinely one-off lines: because the marker exempts the
#     WHOLE line, every extra marked line is another place a second, accidental
#     address can ride along unseen.
#
# Status-roster rule opt-out (section 5): put the token  pii-ok  in a comment on the
# line. Same syntax and same word-boundary guards as ip-ok. It is for a row that is
# already sanitized but must KEEP its column shape because the shape is the subject
# (docs/notes/ carries one) — never for a row still holding a real gamertag or guid.
#
# Password/key rule opt-out (section 2): put the token  pw-ok  in a comment on the
# line. Same syntax and same word-boundary guards as ip-ok / pii-ok. It exempts the
# line from the three password patterns AND the key pattern together, because a
# documented cfg snippet routinely shows both shapes.
#   ⚠ PREFER an EMPTY value — `set rcon_password ""` — which every password rule
#     already allows and which needs no marker at all (server/dedicated.cfg.example
#     documents the cfg exactly that way). Reserve pw-ok for a line that must show a
#     NON-EMPTY shape to make its point, e.g. an indented
#     `set rcon_password <YOUR-PASSWORD-HERE>` inside a markdown list or fenced block.
#   Without it the only escape was `git commit --no-verify`, which disarms the
#   password, key, store, Discord, IP and roster rules all at once — the exact cost
#   release_common.ps1 records this project already paying once, when the roster rule
#   shipped with no opt-out and made its own sanitized documentation uncommittable.
# ⚠ pw-ok is deliberately NOT chained into the IP or roster rules (it is derived from
#   $added, not from $ip_src), so marking a documented cfg snippet cannot also blind
#   the address rule on that line. Same separation as ip-ok vs pii-ok.
# ⚠ It has NO twin in release_common.ps1, and that is correct: that file's Rule B is
#   the twin of sections 4 + 5 only and carries no password or key rule at all, so
#   there is nothing on that side to keep in lockstep. If one is ever added there it
#   must honour pw-ok on these same terms.
#
# Bypass (only if you are SURE a match is a false positive):  git commit --no-verify

fail=0
note() { echo "  pre-commit: $1" >&2; fail=1; }

# 1) Never allow the secret stores / live cfg to be committed, even with `git add -f`.
#
# ⚠ The store patterns are GLOBS on both sides of the name, not "ends with". An exact
#    tail match (`*secrets.local.json`) let a near-miss filename through all three
#    layers at once — `secrets.local.json.bak` was invisible here, invisible to
#    .gitignore's exact paths, and NOT dropped by the packagers, so a routine backup
#    shipped live rcon passwords to another box. Keep the globs in lockstep with
#    .gitignore (`tools/rcon/secrets.local.*`) and with release_common.ps1's
#    $LocalOnlyGlobs — a name missing from any one of the three defeats the other two.
# ⚠ The `*.example` case must stay FIRST: `case` takes the first match, and the
#    committable templates (which exist precisely to document these stores) all end
#    that way. Same guard as release_common.ps1's Test-LocalOnlyPath.
#
# ⚠ EVERY name in release_common.ps1's $LocalOnlyFiles / $LocalOnlyGlobs must appear
#    below. Nine of them once did not — including the `ops.local.*` glob, which
#    tools/hooks/ip-allow.txt's own header names as the sole home of the home egress
#    IP and the VNC console address — so `git add -f tools/ops.local.md` committed
#    clean while deploy would have refused to publish it. Rule A matched them by LEAF
#    NAME, so the exact-name cases here are spelled `*/NAME|NAME` to mean the same
#    thing on a repo-relative path. Where the two still differ, the HOOK is the
#    stricter side by construction (`*secrets.local.*` matches the substring anywhere
#    in the path, Rule A's `secrets.local.*` only a leaf) — stricter-on-entry is the
#    safe direction, since anything this blocks was never going to be publishable.
#
# ⚠ ONE INTENTIONAL ASYMMETRY, DO NOT "FIX" IT: `dedicated.cfg` is blocked here but is
#    deliberately ALLOWED by Rule A, because package_server.ps1 ships the live cfg
#    inside the PRIVATE VPS bundle on purpose (that is where the rcon password is
#    meant to travel, and -RotateRcon rewrites it there). The rule is "never into
#    git", not "never into an output". Every OTHER name below is blocked on both
#    sides.
for p in $(git diff --cached --name-only); do
  case "$p" in
    *.example)                      : ;;   # sanitized template, never the live store
    *secrets.local.*)               note "refusing to commit secret store: $p" ;;
    *servers.local.*)               note "refusing to commit secret store: $p" ;;
    *ops.local.*)                   note "refusing to commit ops crib sheet: $p" ;;
    */prefs.local.json|prefs.local.json)
                                    note "refusing to commit box-local panel state: $p" ;;
    */ignore.local.json|ignore.local.json)
                                    note "refusing to commit muted-player store (GUIDs): $p" ;;
    */security.local.json|security.local.json)
                                    note "refusing to commit trust store: $p" ;;
    */security_state.json|security_state.json)
                                    note "refusing to commit trust-store state: $p" ;;
    */.dvarcache.json|.dvarcache.json)
                                    note "refusing to commit box-local dvar cache: $p" ;;
    */.geocache.json|.geocache.json)
                                    note "refusing to commit geo cache (player IPs): $p" ;;
    */watchdog_state.json|watchdog_state.json)
                                    note "refusing to commit box-local watchdog state: $p" ;;
    */watchdog_maintenance.json|watchdog_maintenance.json)
                                    note "refusing to commit box-local watchdog state: $p" ;;
    */config.json|config.json)      note "refusing to commit notifier secret: $p" ;;
    dedicated.cfg|*/dedicated.cfg)  note "refusing to commit live server cfg: $p" ;;
  esac
done

# 2) Scan ADDED lines of the staged diff for secret literals. Only added ('+') lines,
#    excluding this hook + the IP allowlist (its whole job is to hold addresses).
#
# ⚠ `.example` templates are DELIBERATELY scanned. They used to be excluded here so
#    they could document a pattern, but that exclusion fed EVERY content rule below
#    (passwords, keys, Discord, IP, roster) — making `*.example` the one file class
#    the guard refused to look at, while the templates whose entire job is to show an
#    operator where the real rcon password / home egress IP / VPS address go are
#    exactly that class. A template that must show a pattern carries the per-line
#    marker (ip-ok / pii-ok), which is the mechanism already built for this.
added=$(git diff --cached -U0 --no-color -- . ':(exclude)tools/hooks/pre-commit' \
        ':(exclude)tools/hooks/ip-allow.txt' \
        | grep '^+' | grep -v '^+++')

# rcon_password / g_password ASSIGNED a NON-EMPTY value (blank "" and prose are allowed).
#   cfg form :  set rcon_password "value"  |  set rcon_password 'value'  |  set rcon_password value
#   gsc form :  setDvar( "rcon_password", "value" )
#
# ⚠ Bare and single-quoted values are valid cfg syntax and used to walk straight
#    through a rule that only looked for double quotes. They are matched by a SECOND,
#    LINE-ANCHORED pattern (optionally behind a `//` comment, so a commented-out live
#    line is still caught) rather than by widening the first: unanchored, "set
#    rcon_password" occurs mid-sentence in prose ("...dev block set rcon_password
#    live - DISPROVEN...") and every such mention would block a docs commit.
#
# Per-line opt-out for THIS section (both the password patterns and the key pattern
# below): the token  pw-ok . Documented in the header. Derived from $added and NOT
# chained onto $ip_src, so a pw-ok line carrying a real address is still caught by
# section 4 — marking a documented cfg snippet must never blind the address rule.
pw_src=$(printf '%s\n' "$added" | grep -vE '(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_-])pw-ok([^A-Za-z0-9_-]|$)')

pw_cfg='(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_])(rcon_password|g_password)[[:space:]]+"[^"]+"'
# ⚠ The leading slot is '\+?' (the diff's own '+') plus an OPTIONAL markdown list /
#   blockquote marker — same lesson as the roster regex in section 5, found the same
#   way. It was '.?', which the '+' consumes, so the marker had nothing left to match
#   with: `- set rcon_password <value>` — a bare value in a markdown LIST ITEM, which
#   is precisely the shape the pw-ok note above is about — walked through this rule at
#   exit 0. Only the bare/single-quoted form escaped; the double-quoted form is caught
#   by the unanchored $pw_cfg, and the plain-indented form was already caught.
#   The rest of the anchoring is still deliberate and must stay: unanchored, "set
#   rcon_password" occurs mid-sentence in prose and every such mention would block a
#   docs commit. A bullet marker is not a word, so allowing one keeps prose out.
pw_bare='^\+?[[:space:]]*([-*>|][[:space:]]*)?(//[[:space:]]*)?seta?[[:space:]]+(rcon_password|g_password)[[:space:]]+([^[:space:]'"'"'"]|'"'"'[^'"'"']+'"'"')'
pw_dvar='"(rcon_password|g_password)"[[:space:]]*,[[:space:]]*"[^"]+"'
if printf '%s\n' "$pw_src" | grep -Eiq -e "$pw_cfg" -e "$pw_bare" -e "$pw_dvar"; then
  note "a non-empty rcon_password/g_password value is staged:"
  printf '%s\n' "$pw_src" | grep -Ein -e "$pw_cfg" -e "$pw_bare" -e "$pw_dvar" >&2
  echo "  -> if it is a documented placeholder: prefer \`set rcon_password \"\"\`, or put 'pw-ok' on the line" >&2
fi

# Plutonium server key / any long key token:  key<sep><20+ char token>
key_re='key["'"'"'= ]+[A-Za-z0-9+/=_-]{20,}'
if printf '%s\n' "$pw_src" | grep -Eiq "$key_re"; then
  note "a long key-like token is staged (Plutonium server key?):"
  printf '%s\n' "$pw_src" | grep -Ein "$key_re" >&2
  echo "  -> if it is a documented placeholder shape: put 'pw-ok' on the line" >&2
fi

# 3) Discord invite must ALWAYS be the canonical vanity URL discord.gg/blackops.
#    Raw/temporary invite codes expire; a stale one shipped a dead link on the
#    public website once. Any other discord.gg/<code> is blocked.
bad_dg=$(printf '%s\n' "$added" | grep -oiE 'discord\.gg/[A-Za-z0-9]+' | grep -viE '^discord\.gg/blackops$')
if [ -n "$bad_dg" ]; then
  note "a non-canonical Discord invite is staged (must be https://discord.gg/blackops):"
  printf '%s\n' "$bad_dg" | sort -u >&2
fi

# 4) Public IP literals. A pasted `status` reply once put a third party's real IP
#    into docs/, and the RDP/SSH firewall runbook published the owner's home egress
#    IP — the two worst leaks in this repo, and neither is a "secret" any password
#    scanner would catch. Flag any PUBLIC IPv4/IPv6 literal on an added line.
#
#    Deliberately NOT flagged: loopback / private / link-local / CGNAT-Tailscale /
#    multicast / broadcast, the RFC 5737 + RFC 3849 documentation ranges, and any
#    dotted quad whose four octets are ALL <= 20 — that shape is indistinguishable
#    from a version string (1.2.3.4, 6.4.0.1), so it is conceded. ⚠ The cost of
#    that concession is real but tiny: a public address with every octet <= 20
#    (8.8.8.8, 1.1.1.1) walks through. No player or home IP has ever had that
#    shape; version strings constantly do.
ip_allow="$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)/tools/hooks/ip-allow.txt"

# Drop the lines the author marked, then reduce what is left to bare digit/dot
# tokens. Whole-token matching is what makes '1.2.3.4.5', '10.0.19045.123' and a
# '\d{1,3}(\.\d{1,3}){3}' regex literal all fail to match (this repo has six of
# those regexes), and what lets two addresses on ONE line both be seen.
ip_src=$(printf '%s\n' "$added" | grep -vE '(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_-])ip-ok([^A-Za-z0-9_-]|$)')

# ⚠ The `sed` is load-bearing, and it is the twin of release_common.ps1's
#   `-replace '\.*$'` in Get-LeakIpLiterals — change both or neither.
#   '.' is INSIDE the tr character class (it has to be, it is the quad separator), so
#   a SENTENCE-ENDING period is absorbed into the token: 'answered from <addr>.' came
#   out as a 5-group token, failed the whole-quad grep, and BOTH guards reported
#   nothing — while the same address with any following word was caught. This rule's
#   whole stated purpose is human-authored prose, and end-of-sentence is the single
#   most common prose position for an address, so that was the biggest hole in it.
#   Strips ALL trailing dots, not one, so a trailing ellipsis is covered too; the PS
#   side does the same. It cannot manufacture a false positive: removing dots can only
#   REDUCE the group count, and every survivor still has to pass the whole-quad grep
#   and the full exclusion ladder below ('...on 127.0.0.1.' is still skipped).
bad_ip4=$(printf '%s\n' "$ip_src" \
  | tr -c '0-9.' '\n' \
  | sed 's/\.*$//' \
  | grep -E '^[0-9]{1,3}(\.[0-9]{1,3}){3}$' \
  | sort -u \
  | while IFS=. read -r a b c d; do
      [ "$a" -gt 255 ] || [ "$b" -gt 255 ] || [ "$c" -gt 255 ] || [ "$d" -gt 255 ] && continue
      [ "$a" -le 20 ] && [ "$b" -le 20 ] && [ "$c" -le 20 ] && [ "$d" -le 20 ] && continue
      case "$a.$b" in
        0.*|10.*|127.*)                  continue ;;   # this-network / private / loopback
        169.254)                         continue ;;   # link-local
        172.1[6-9]|172.2[0-9]|172.3[01]) continue ;;   # private 172.16/12
        192.168)                         continue ;;   # private
        100.6[4-9]|100.[7-9][0-9]|100.1[01][0-9]|100.12[0-7]) continue ;;  # CGNAT / Tailscale
        198.1[89])                       continue ;;   # RFC 2544 benchmarking
        192.0)   [ "$c" -eq 2 ]   && continue ;;       # RFC 5737 TEST-NET-1
        198.51)  [ "$c" -eq 100 ] && continue ;;       # RFC 5737 TEST-NET-2
        203.0)   [ "$c" -eq 113 ] && continue ;;       # RFC 5737 TEST-NET-3
      esac
      [ "$a" -ge 224 ] && continue                     # multicast / reserved / broadcast
      echo "$a.$b.$c.$d"
    done)

# IPv6: only a GLOBAL-UNICAST literal (first group 2xxx/3xxx) carrying a '::' or a
# 4th group. That anchor alone excludes ::1, ::, fe80::, fc00::/fd00::, ff02::,
# clock times (14:22:07), dates (2026:07:25) and the mod's own colon-packed
# telemetry strings ('0:0:1:0:0:gf', '3:2:5:2:1:gf:0:3:3:3:1:fu').
bad_ip6=$(printf '%s\n' "$ip_src" \
  | tr -c '0-9A-Fa-f:' '\n' \
  | grep -E '^[23][0-9A-Fa-f]{3}(:[0-9A-Fa-f]{0,4}){2,7}$' \
  | grep -E '::|^([0-9A-Fa-f]*:){3}' \
  | grep -viE '^2001:0*db8:' \
  | sort -u)

bad_ip=$(printf '%s\n%s\n' "$bad_ip4" "$bad_ip6" | grep -v '^$' | sort -u)
if [ -n "$bad_ip" ] && [ -f "$ip_allow" ]; then
  allow=$(sed 's/#.*//' "$ip_allow" | tr -d ' \t' | grep -v '^$' | sort -u)
  [ -n "$allow" ] && bad_ip=$(printf '%s\n' "$bad_ip" | grep -Fxv "$allow")
fi
if [ -n "$bad_ip" ]; then
  note "a public IP literal is staged (player PII / infrastructure disclosure):"
  printf '%s\n' "$bad_ip" | sed 's/^/    /' >&2
  printf '%s\n' "$ip_src" | grep -F "$bad_ip" >&2
  echo "  -> if intentional: put 'ip-ok' on the line, or list it in tools/hooks/ip-allow.txt" >&2
fi

# 5) A pasted `status` reply is exactly how the player PII got in. Its address column
# is already covered above; this catches a row whose IP was scrubbed but whose
# guid + gamertag were not. Signature = the 4 leading integer columns
# (num score ping guid) followed by a name carrying a Treyarch '^N' colour code.
# Narrow on purpose: a bare guid is a 7-digit integer and cannot be matched
# without flagging every timestamp and byte count in the repo.
#
# The '[[:space:]]*' after the diff prefix is load-bearing: the '+' is consumed by the
# leading-character slot, so without it a single leading space (a row nested inside a
# markdown list) walked through the whole rule.
#
# ⚠ THE LEADING-CHARACTER SLOT IS THE ONE PLACE THIS REGEX AND ITS TWIN ARE
#   DELIBERATELY NOT SPELLED THE SAME, because they are fed different input:
#   THIS side matches DIFF lines, which always carry a '+' prefix — hence '\+?'.
#   release_common.ps1's $StatusRosterRe matches RAW file lines, which do not — hence
#   a bare '^\s*'. Both therefore mean the identical thing: *the row's own first
#   character must be whitespace or a digit*.
#   Both used to be written '.?' "so the two spellings stay diffable". Identical text
#   over different input is not identical behaviour: on the PS side that '.?' was free
#   to eat one ARBITRARY character, so a roster row opening with a markdown bullet
#   ('- '), a table pipe ('| ') or a blockquote ('> ') was PASSED here and REFUSED by
#   Find-LeakHits — precisely the "hook waves it through, deploy refuses it at 2am"
#   split that release_common.ps1's own header says must never exist.
#   Keep them SEMANTICALLY equal, not textually equal. The residual is shared and
#   known: a roster row whose first character is '-', '|' or '>' is out of scope for
#   BOTH guards now. That was already true of the guard that governs entry into git,
#   so nothing regressed — and a bulleted row still trips the IP rule above unless its
#   address was scrubbed too.
#
# Own opt-out token, because the two rules answer different questions: a row can be
# correctly sanitized (RFC 5737 address, invented name and guid) and still need to
# KEEP the roster shape, because in that note the shape IS the subject. Deliberately
# NOT an exemption for rows whose address is a documentation range — that would blind
# the rule to the one thing it is here for, a row whose IP was scrubbed while the
# gamertag and guid were not.
#
# ⚠ Note the one-way asymmetry, and leave it: 'pii-ok' does NOT exempt a line from the
#   IP rule, but 'ip-ok' DOES exempt it from this one (status_src is derived from
#   ip_src). release_common.ps1 has the same shape — its `continue` on ip-ok skips the
#   roster check too — so this is lockstep, not an oversight. Fix one side alone and
#   the two guards start disagreeing about the same line.
status_src=$(printf '%s\n' "$ip_src" | grep -vE '(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_-])pii-ok([^A-Za-z0-9_-]|$)')

bad_status=$(printf '%s\n' "$status_src" | grep -E '^\+?[[:space:]]*[0-9]+ +-?[0-9]+ +[0-9]+ +[0-9]+ +[^ ]*\^[0-9]')
if [ -n "$bad_status" ]; then
  note "an added line looks like a pasted 'status' roster row (gamertag + guid = player PII):"
  printf '%s\n' "$bad_status" | sed 's/^/    /' >&2
  echo "  -> if the row is already sanitized and must keep its shape: put 'pii-ok' on the line" >&2
fi

if [ "$fail" -ne 0 ]; then
  echo "" >&2
  echo "  Commit blocked to keep secrets out of git. Fix the lines above," >&2
  echo "  or bypass with:  git commit --no-verify  (only if truly a false positive)." >&2
  exit 1
fi
exit 0
